// Expected: a0a0 at one session and a1a0 at two.
//
// Bob receives the MACed pair unguarded in phase 0 and relays it to Carol
// under guards in phase 1, after the key has leaked. Carol's delivery is in
// phase 1, but a guard admits only the substitution made upstream, on the
// way to Bob, and that happened in phase 0 with no key in hand: Bob's check
// would have refused it. So the phase that governs a guarded relay is the
// phase of the unguarded delivery it stands for, and the phase-1 key forges
// nothing here.
//
// At two sessions the open leg into Bob takes the other session's pair,
// which his check accepts because the key is shared, and that is a genuine
// duplicate acceptance of Alice's sending. The relayed leg is not: Bob
// forwards what he himself accepted, once, and Carol takes it once.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	generates x
	t = MAC(k, x)
]

Alice -> Bob: x, t

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	_ = ASSERT(MAC(k, x), t)?
]

phase[1]

principal Alice[
	leaks k
]

Bob -> Carol: [x], [t]

principal Carol[
	knows private k
	_ = ASSERT(MAC(k, x), t)?
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: x
	authentication? Bob -> Carol: x
]
